\ A promoted arithmetic result meeting a parameter the freeze-time inference
\ proved fixnum. Under the lock the pass types `a + b` on two fixnums as fixnum,
\ and `take`'s parameter is narrowed from that. The cold arm promotes on
\ overflow, so the proof can be false at runtime.
\
\ The proof is marked declared for exactly this reason, which puts a tag check at
\ `take`'s entry. The check traps instead of reading the bignum's payload as an
\ i64, and names the word, the type it proved, and the value that arrived.

take: ( n -- ) [ 1 + drop "took it" print-line ] ;

pick-huge: ( b -- n ) [ [ 9223372036854775807 ] [ 3 ] if ] ;

f pick-huge 1 + take
t pick-huge 1 + take
